  [1mThe fork:[0m where a reminder physically appears.

  The constraint: with no server, [1ma reminder can only fire while a tab of this app is open.[0m Closed tab, closed browser,
  phone in pocket — nothing fires. Waking a closed app requires a push server, which "browser-only" rules out. A service
  worker doesn't rescue this; without push subscriptions the browser won't reliably wake it. So whatever we pick, "Pay
  rent due in 1 hour" arrives only if the app happens to be open at that moment.

  - [1mIn-page only[0m — a banner/toast plus an "overdue" badge in the list. No permission prompt, works everywhere, zero API
    risk. But if the tab is in the background, the user sees it whenever they next look — which, given the constraint
    above, is arguably the honest behavior anyway.
  - [1mIn-page + Web Notifications API[0m — same thing, plus an OS-level notification when the tab is backgrounded. Costs a
    permission prompt (and a denial path you have to handle gracefully forever). Genuinely better when the app is open
    in a background tab all day, which for a task app is a realistic usage pattern.
  - [1mIn-page + catch-up on load[0m — in-page, plus on startup the app reports what you missed while it was closed: "3 tasks
    went overdue since you were last here." Directly addresses the closed-tab gap instead of pretending it isn't there.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-page + catch-up on load, with the Notifications API as a later opt-in.[0m Catch-up is the piece
  that makes the feature survive its own limitation — it's the difference between a reminder system and one that
  silently drops anything that happened overnight. The OS-level layer is a small, additive change once the core exists,
  and deferring it keeps a permission prompt out of the first version.

